[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Component Lifecycle Methods (Class Components)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In React, components have a lifecycle, which is a series of events that occur from the moment a component is created and mounted on the DOM to the moment it is unmounted and removed from the DOM. Class components provide access to specific lifecycle methods that allow you to hook into these events and perform actions at different stages of a component's existence. Understanding these methods is crucial for managing component state, handling side effects, and optimizing performance."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Mounting"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mounting refers to the process of adding a component to the DOM. The following lifecycle methods are invoked in order during the mounting phase:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**constructor()**: This is the first method called when a component is created. It's used to initialize the component's state and bind event handlers. If you don't initialize state and you don't bind methods, you don't need to implement a constructor for your React component."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**static getDerivedStateFromProps()**: This method is invoked before rendering, both on the initial mount and subsequent updates. It should return an object to update the state, or null to indicate that the new props do not require any state updates. It's rarely used."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**render()**: This method is required and is responsible for describing the UI. It should be a pure function of `props` and `state`, meaning it should always return the same result for the same inputs and should not directly modify the component's state or interact with the browser. It can return React elements, arrays and fragments, booleans or null, and strings or numbers."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**componentDidMount()**: This method is invoked immediately after a component is mounted (inserted into the DOM tree). It's commonly used to perform side effects, such as fetching data from an API, setting up subscriptions, or directly manipulating the DOM."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"class MyComponent extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { data: null };
  }

  componentDidMount() {
    fetch('https://api.example.com/data')
      .then(response => response.json())
      .then(data => this.setState({ data }));
  }

  render() {
    if (!this.state.data) {
      return <p>Loading...</p>;
    }
    return <p>Data: {this.state.data.value}</p>;
  }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Updating"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Updating occurs when a component's props or state change, causing it to re-render. The following lifecycle methods are invoked during the updating phase:"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**static getDerivedStateFromProps()**: As mentioned before, this method is also invoked before rendering during updates."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**shouldComponentUpdate()**: This method is invoked before rendering when new props or state are being received. It allows you to control whether a component should re-render. It should return `true` to update the component or `false` to skip the update. It's primarily used for performance optimization."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**render()**: As before, this method is responsible for describing the UI based on the current `props` and `state`."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**getSnapshotBeforeUpdate()**: This method is invoked right before the DOM is updated. It allows you to capture information from the DOM (e.g., scroll position) before it's potentially changed. The value returned by this method will be passed as a parameter to `componentDidUpdate()`."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**componentDidUpdate()**: This method is invoked immediately after an update occurs. It's commonly used to perform side effects based on the previous and current props or state. You can also perform DOM operations here. However, you should avoid updating the state within this method without a conditional check, as it can lead to an infinite loop."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"class MyComponent extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { count: 0 };
  }

  shouldComponentUpdate(nextProps, nextState) {
    // Only update if the count has changed
    return nextState.count !== this.state.count;
  }

  getSnapshotBeforeUpdate(prevProps, prevState) {
    // Capture the scroll position before the update
    this.scrollPosition = document.getElementById('myDiv').scrollTop;
    return null;
  }

  componentDidUpdate(prevProps, prevState, snapshot) {
    // Restore the scroll position after the update
    if (snapshot !== null) {
      document.getElementById('myDiv').scrollTop = this.scrollPosition;
    }
  }

  handleClick = () => {
    this.setState(prevState => ({ count: prevState.count + 1 }));
  };

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <div id="myDiv" style={{ height: '100px', overflow: 'auto' }}>
          {/* Some content here */}
          <p>Count: {this.state.count}</p>
        </div>
        <button onClick={this.handleClick}>Increment</button>
      </div>
    );
  }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Unmounting"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unmounting occurs when a component is removed from the DOM. The following lifecycle method is invoked during the unmounting phase:"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**componentWillUnmount()**: This method is invoked immediately before a component is unmounted and destroyed. It's commonly used to perform cleanup tasks, such as canceling network requests, removing event listeners, or invalidating timers."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"class MyComponent extends React.Component {
  componentDidMount() {
    this.intervalId = setInterval(() => {
      console.log('Tick');
    }, 1000);
  }

  componentWillUnmount() {
    clearInterval(this.intervalId);
  }

  render() {
    return <p>Component is running</p>;
  }
}"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Error Handling"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"React also provides lifecycle methods for handling errors within components:"},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**static getDerivedStateFromError()**: This method is invoked after an error has been thrown by a descendant component. It receives the error that was thrown as an argument and should return a value to update the state."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**componentDidCatch()**: This method is invoked after an error has been thrown by a descendant component. It receives the error that was thrown and information about which component threw the error. It's commonly used to log error information or display a fallback UI."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { hasError: false };
  }

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {
    // Update state so the next render will show the fallback UI.
    return { hasError: true };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error, errorInfo) {
    // You can also log the error to an error reporting service
    console.error(error, errorInfo);
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      // You can render any custom fallback UI
      return <h1>Something went wrong.</h1>;
    }

    return this.props.children; 
  }
}"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"By understanding and utilizing these lifecycle methods, you can effectively manage component behavior, handle side effects, and optimize performance in your React applications."}]
